{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a nullable `word_count` column to `entries` (new migration at the end of the list) and expose it as `word_count` on the API entry object (`*int`, null where unknown).\n\n- The count is computed in the storage layer on every content write: `createEntry`, `updateEntry` (feed refresh) and `UpdateEntryTitleAndContent` (API edit / scraper fetch). It is `len(strings.Fields(sanitizer.StripTags(content)))`.\n- Existing entries are not backfilled; they stay null until their content is next saved.\n- `fetchEntries` now selects and scans `e.word_count`; the Go client's `Entry` also gained `WordCount`.\n\nOne behaviour to be aware of: `StripTags` concatenates text across adjacent tags without inserting whitespace, so `<p>one</p><p>two</p>` counts as 1 word. This matches how the existing reading-time estimate strips tags.\n\nExisting unit tests (53 packages) and integration tests (102) pass; the new behaviour itself has no test of its own.", "t": 1794462671.035}
